=base:summary.cmark
Numbers are now two types, an integer and a float. This page lists what changes for existing templates and hosts, and what to do about each.

=base:main-content.cmark

# Migrating to integers and floats

tramaj had one number type, a double. It now has two, an **integer** and a
**float**, and nothing converts between them unless the template says so.
The same change brings arithmetic, as nine named builtins.

Arithmetic is an addition: a host turns it on, and a template that does not
use it is unaffected. The number split is not optional, and it changes what
some existing templates and hosts produce. This page covers the split.

## Who is affected

<table>
<tr><th>implementation</th><th>number split</th><th>arithmetic</th><th>integer range</th></tr>
<tr><td><code>tramaj-purs</code>, and <code>tramaj-halogen</code>, <code>tramaj-cli</code> and the playground built on it</td><td>yes</td><td>yes</td><td><code>-(2^53 - 1)</code> to <code>2^53 - 1</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tramaj-hs</code></td><td>yes</td><td>yes</td><td>signed 64-bit</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tramaj-go</code></td><td>yes</td><td>yes</td><td>signed 64-bit</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tramaj-rs</code>, <code>tramaj-cli-rs</code>, <code>tramaj-js</code>, <code>tramaj-react</code>, <code>tramaj-py</code></td><td>not yet</td><td>not yet</td><td>numbers are doubles</td></tr>
</table>

The PureScript, Haskell and Rust changes are on the `main` branch and in no
release yet. A host that follows `main` as a git dependency gets them on its
next update. The other three implementations still behave as before, and will
change the same way when they get the split.

## The changes at a glance

<table>
<tr><th>what</th><th>before</th><th>now</th></tr>
<tr><td><code>str</code> of a whole-valued float</td><td><code>str(1.0)</code> is <code>1</code></td><td><code>str(1.0)</code> is <code>1.0</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>eq</code> across the two types</td><td><code>eq(1, 1.0)</code> is <code>true</code></td><td><code>false</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>lt</code>, <code>lte</code>, <code>gt</code>, <code>gte</code> across the two types</td><td><code>gt(1.5, 0)</code> is <code>true</code></td><td>a <code>TypeMismatch</code></td></tr>
<tr><td>a JSON number in the context</td><td>a double, whatever its text</td><td><code>3</code> is an integer, <code>3.0</code> and <code>3e0</code> are floats</td></tr>
<tr><td>an integer outside the range</td><td>rounded to the nearest double</td><td>refused: a parse error as a literal, a <code>TypeMismatch</code> in the context</td></tr>
<tr><td>the object key <code>"$term"</code></td><td>an ordinary key</td><td>reserved, like <code>"$sym"</code> and <code>"$type"</code></td></tr>
<tr><td>the type primitive <code>number</code> (v4)</td><td>a primitive</td><td>gone: <code>int</code> and <code>float</code> replace it</td></tr>
</table>

## 1. A whole-valued float is written with a fraction

A number keeps its type when it is written. An integer is written as its
digits. A float always carries a fraction or an exponent, so a float with no
fractional part gets `.0`:

<table>
<tr><th>value</th><th>before</th><th>now</th></tr>
<tr><td><code>1</code></td><td><code>1</code></td><td><code>1</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>1.0</code></td><td><code>1</code></td><td><code>1.0</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>1e5</code></td><td><code>100000</code></td><td><code>100000.0</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>1.5</code></td><td><code>1.5</code></td><td><code>1.5</code></td></tr>
</table>

This applies to `str`, to string interpolation, and to every number in the
JSON an evaluation returns: text values, attribute values, action payloads,
value slots, and an expression program's result.

**What to do.** Look for float literals that are whole (`1.0`, `1e5`) and for
context numbers a producer writes with a fraction (`"ratio": 2.0`). Where
the text matters and the value is a count, make it an integer: write the
literal `100000`, or have the producer write `2`. An integer renders exactly
as before.

A consumer that reads the output with an ordinary JSON parser sees the same
numeric value for `3` and `3.0`. Only a consumer that compares the output as
text, or that decodes it back into a `Node`, sees the difference.

## 2. Comparisons do not mix the two types

`eq` never equates an integer with a float, and the four ordering
comparisons take two integers or two floats:

```
eq(1, 1.0)         -- false, was true
gt($ctx.ratio, 0)  -- TypeMismatch when ratio is a float
```

The first of these is silent: a `branch` or a `filter` that compared a
context number with a literal of the other type now takes the other arm.

**What to do.** Write the literal in the type of what it is compared with:
`gt($ctx.ratio, 0.0)` for a float, `eq($ctx.count, 0)` for an integer.

When the template does not know which type its host sends, and the
implementation has arithmetic turned on, normalize with one of the two
conversions. Each accepts both types:

```
gt(real($ctx.ratio), 0.0)   -- real: the float nearest to its argument
eq(floor($ctx.count), 0)    -- floor: the largest integer not above it
```

## 3. A JSON number is typed by its text

A number in the context is read as the literal of the same text. With
neither a fraction nor an exponent it is an integer; with either it is a
float. So `{"n": 3}` and `{"n": 3.0}` are two different contexts.

Two things follow for a host.

**The producer of the context decides the type.** A serializer with a single
number type writes the float `3.0` as `3`, and the template then receives an
integer. If a template needs a float from such a producer, it normalizes
with `real`, as above.

**The host's JSON parser must keep the difference.** JavaScript's
`JSON.parse` and Haskell's `aeson` `Value` both lose it. The two
implementations therefore ship their own JSON value, and their public API
takes it in place of the previous one:

<table>
<tr><th></th><th>PureScript</th><th>Haskell</th></tr>
<tr><td>module</td><td><code>Tramaj.Json</code></td><td><code>Tramaj.Json</code></td></tr>
<tr><td>replaces</td><td><code>Data.Argonaut.Core.Json</code></td><td><code>Data.Aeson.Value</code></td></tr>
<tr><td>read text</td><td><code>jsonParser</code></td><td><code>jsonParser</code></td></tr>
<tr><td>write text</td><td><code>stringify</code></td><td><code>stringify</code></td></tr>
<tr><td>bridge</td><td>none: build values with <code>fromInt</code>, <code>fromFloat</code>, …</td><td><code>fromAeson</code>, <code>toAeson</code></td></tr>
</table>

The Haskell bridge cannot recover what `aeson` dropped. `fromAeson` makes
every whole number that fits 64 bits an integer, and `toAeson` gives the type
up again. Read the JSON text with `jsonParser` when the difference matters.

**Numbers that are not values are refused.** An integer-form number outside
the implementation's integer range is not rounded any more: it is a parse
error as a literal and a `TypeMismatch` in the context. So is a float too
large for a double, such as `1e400`. The context is checked whole before
evaluation starts, so the error does not depend on whether the template
reads that number. `-0` and `-0.0` are read as zero.

The integer range differs between the two implementations (see the table at
the top). Inside `-(2^53 - 1)` to `2^53 - 1` they agree. A larger integer
that the Haskell implementation emits is refused by the PureScript one.

## 4. `"$term"` is a reserved key

Over a symbol, an arithmetic builtin does not compute. It evaluates to a
*term*, written as an object with the keys `"$term"` and `"arguments"`. To
keep that unambiguous, `"$term"` joins `"$sym"` and `"$type"` as a reserved
key, whether or not arithmetic is turned on:

- an object literal with the key `"$term"` in a template is a parse error;
- a context that carries the key is a `TypeMismatch`. The one exception is a
  well-formed term seeded in symbolic mode, with arithmetic turned on.

**What to do.** Rename the key in any data that happens to use it.

## 5. `number` is no longer a type (v4)

In a type annotation, `int` and `float` replace the primitive `number`:

```
@n : int = 3        -- was: @n : number = 3
@r : float = 0.5
```

`number` is now an unresolved type name. A host checker that received
`{"$type": "number"}` receives `{"$type": "int"}` or `{"$type": "float"}`.

## What does not change

- The grammar. There are no new operators and no new syntax.
- A template that uses only integers, and whose context holds only numbers
  written without a fraction or an exponent, evaluates to the same output.
- `evalProgram` and `runProgram` keep their signatures, apart from the JSON
  type above. They run without arithmetic.

## Arithmetic is separate, and off by default

The nine builtins (`sum`, `product`, `negate`, `quotient`, `inverse`,
`floor-quotient`, `modulo`, `floor`, `real`) are an optional profile that a
host chooses per evaluation. Without it the names are unbound, so an existing
template that binds its own `sum` or `floor` keeps working either way: a
binding shadows the builtin.

<table>
<tr><th>where</th><th>how to turn it on</th></tr>
<tr><td><code>tramaj-purs</code></td><td><code>runProgramWith (defaultOptions { arithmetic = true })</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tramaj-hs</code></td><td><code>runProgramWith defaultOptions { optArithmetic = True }</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>tramaj-cli</code></td><td><code>tramaj-cli --arithmetic template.tramaj context.json</code></td></tr>
<tr><td>playground</td><td>always on</td></tr>
</table>

`tramaj-cli analyze arithmetic template.tramaj` lists the arithmetic names a
template and its libraries reference, without running anything. A host
without the profile can use it to refuse a template up front.

## Where the rules are written

- [Language reference](/reference-language.html), sections 3 (values), 5
  (number literals), 6 (`str`), 11 (comparisons and arithmetic) and 13 (the
  integer range).
- [Node JSON representation](/reference-node-json.html), *Numbers*.
- [Symbols reference](/reference-v3-symbols.html), section 1.9 (terms) and
  5.3 (reserved keys).
- [`decisions.md`](https://github.com/lucasdicioccio/tramaj/blob/main/specs/decisions.md),
  section 18, for the reasoning and the alternatives that were rejected.

